diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation finds no fault anywhere in the network. Every
subsystem was tested and is working correctly.

DNS: The BIND server on dns_server correctly resolves web1.local to 10.200.0.4
(web_server_1's address), matching the zone record in /etc/bind/db.local.

IP reachability: ICMP ping from host_1_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.4 completes 4/4 packets
with 0% loss and TTL=60, consistent with the four L3 hops through the OSPF domain
(switch_dist_1_1 → router_core_1 → router_core_3 → switch_server_access).

OSPF routing: All core routers carry correct, converged routes. router_core_1 routes
10.200.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.3 (router_core_3) on eth1, not via switch_dist_1_1; the
return path from switch_server_access back to 10.1.1.0/24 is equally clean.

Application layer: Apache on web_server_1 is running and healthy — it returns HTTP
200 with "Welcome to Web Server 1" to a Host: web1.local request issued directly
from host_1_1_1_1. No iptables rules on the server block any traffic. There are no
Apache error-log entries.

The same HTTP test succeeds from host_2_1_1_1 and from direct IP (10.200.0.4),
confirming the service is reachable from all parts of the network.

Root cause: There is no fault. The user's report of a timeout is not reproducible;
the network and web service are operating normally.

Corrective action: No configuration change is needed. The user should clear their
browser cache or try again — the service is live and healthy as of this investigation.
